Oryn on FreeBSD: after installing
=================================

Required: nothing beyond the base system. Everything below is optional and
adds readings.

1. CPU temperatures: load the sensor driver (AMD: amdtemp, Intel: coretemp)

     sudo kldload amdtemp                  # or: coretemp
     sudo sysrc kld_list+="amdtemp"        # keep it after reboot

2. CPU power (watts), RAM temperatures and part numbers: two more kernel
   modules (read as root)

     sudo kldload cpuctl smb
     sudo sysrc kld_list+="cpuctl smb"

3. Optional packages

     sudo pkg install smartmontools        # SATA/NVMe SMART temperatures
     sudo pkg install dmidecode            # RAM model and speed without root
     sudo pkg install nvidia-driver        # NVIDIA GPU panel (nvidia-smi)
     sudo pkg install xrandr               # monitors in the specs panel (X11)

4. Run it

     oryn              # everything readable as a normal user
     sudo oryn         # adds SMART, CPU watts, RAM, NVMe health and
                       # NVIDIA hotspot / VRAM temperatures

   Optional: let a normal `oryn` use smartctl and dmidecode without a
   password (Oryn only ever runs them with `sudo -n`, never asks). The
   rule allows only the read-only commands Oryn runs, not the programs
   with any arguments (as root they can change drive settings or
   overwrite files):

     SMART="/usr/local/sbin/smartctl -j -n standby -i -A"
     printf '%s\n' \
       "$USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: $SMART -d nvme /dev/nvme[0-9], $SMART -d nvme /dev/nvme[1-9][0-9]" \
       "$USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: $SMART -d atacam /dev/ada[0-9], $SMART -d atacam /dev/ada[1-9][0-9]" \
       "$USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: $SMART /dev/da[0-9], $SMART /dev/da[1-9][0-9]" \
       "$USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: $SMART -d sat /dev/da[0-9], $SMART -d sat /dev/da[1-9][0-9]" \
       "$USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/dmidecode -t 17" \
       | sudo tee /usr/local/etc/sudoers.d/oryn >/dev/null
     sudo chmod 440 /usr/local/etc/sudoers.d/oryn
     sudo visudo -cf /usr/local/etc/sudoers.d/oryn

   Device names use [0-9], not *: in sudoers a * in the arguments also
   matches spaces, so it would allow extra arguments.

Check what was found:  oryn --list
Manual:                man oryn
